      "side": 1,
      "message": "The change in the generated MSL/HLSL/GLSL should pretty much mirror the change in the WGSL in these cases. When something is a `let` it generally gets preserved as a named temporary and would get emitted as such in all of the textual shading languages we generate. So the things that get inlined in the WGSL here would likely also be inlined in the generated backend code. There\u0027d be no significant change for SPIR-V though since we just ignore `let` in that backend.\n\nBTW, if size reduction is your goal, a couple of other low-hanging fruit that jump out to me would be more aggressively using type inference (i.e. drop the `: type` from `const`/`let` declarations, and from `var` when possible), and using the built-in type aliases (`vec4\u003cf32\u003e` -\u003e `vec4f`, and `vec3\u003cf32\u003e(a, b, c)` -\u003e `vec3(a, b, c)`).",
